#: ../../source/docs/common-pitot-considerations.rst:7
#: 76c7066f57f448d6afa75fabdf366e57
msgid "Pitot Tube Considerations"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-pitot-considerations.rst:9
#: 18f690a65c5b4cd4895a08b920c4741c
msgid "Airspeed sensor accuracy is very dependent on the placement and construction of the pitot tube and static port. For vehicles that do not operate close to their stall speed, airspeed accuracy is not as critical and pitot/static port configuration and placement are not as critical, but some basic guidelines still need to be followed."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-pitot-considerations.rst:12
#: 40f05bf961144a5aba4153e88b242980
msgid "Pitot Dynamic Pressure Input/Static Port"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-pitot-considerations.rst:14
#: dc72bf85b3cb4e228d17529b56e83cb7
msgid "Airspeed sensors work by measuring the differential pressure between a dynamic port facing the vehicle's relative wind and a static port measuring general prevailing air pressure. Usually, these ports are placed on a single pitot tube."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-pitot-considerations.rst:19
#: 7779ccc0894d4236960bd6151f097c29
msgid "The pitot tube should be placed on the vehicle such that:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-pitot-considerations.rst:21
#: 0b5f21439c7942a89841919060d1ab8b
msgid "The dynamic pressure port and static port do not get turbulence from propellers, or aircraft surfaces (boundary layers of wings, pressure fronts of leading edges, rotors generated by cameras, etc.)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-pitot-considerations.rst:22
#: 1d219906b80e4b6b94fbe834b3540760
msgid "The ports should not be positioned as to be affected by angle of attack changes of the vehicle in its normal operating range of speeds. For example, mounting on the top of the aircraft nose on the fuselage will \"blanket\" the sensor as the angle of attack in flight is increased, leading to inaccurate readings."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-pitot-considerations.rst:24
#: e58f0b6ce25a42a89362c1a1c740bc91
msgid "The ideal location would be extended well beyond the nose of the aircraft or wing leading edge, as shown below:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-pitot-considerations.rst:29
#: 9cbf00719f0444c2aaf315e2540dedfa
msgid "But this does present handling and \"trip\" hazards that must be tolerated. However, such positioning is very important for vehicles operating near their stall point."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-pitot-considerations.rst:31
#: 7febafaa53184977a862debfce84edc9
msgid "For sport aircraft which typically fly at speeds well above their stall point (>25-30% speed margin), fuselage placement such as shown below is more practical, with the downside of some (usually acceptable) speed reading variations during tight turns and full power climbs, etc."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-pitot-considerations.rst:37
#: 65a9c0662acb4cee94b33bcca34c727f
msgid "This one is located out of propeller wash, away from turbulence causing fuselage features, away from surface boundary layer flow, but is still subject to fuselage blanketing on sliding turns which may cause some inaccuracies, but for this sport plane operating well above its stall point, it is not an issue."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-pitot-considerations.rst:40
#: 8e865896b72d40ae8a47876b9297c864
msgid "Pitot Clogging"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-pitot-considerations.rst:42
#: 1d9c88f7a861464690830642a26ff5dc
msgid "Many pitot tubes typically supplied with commercial airspeed sensors have dynamic pressure port opening that are very small. In rain, clouds, or dense fog, water droplets can easily clog the port leading to highly inaccurate sensor readings, or AduPilot declaring the sensor has failed. For operations in these environments, be sure to use a pitot tube that has been designed for these conditions."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-pitot-considerations.rst:45
#: ea30b22f83454a8aa3ced843c9f52ca7
msgid "Cabin Static Port"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-pitot-considerations.rst:47
#: 7f06b47bae2241b18a88db28848db0a8
msgid "Some Ready To Fly vehicles use an internal cabin static port for the airspeed sensor instead of placement on the external pitot tube. These can sometimes provide adequate results, but can easily result in reading inaccuracies if the cabin in which they are located are subject to pressure changes in flight (ie. openings in cabin exposed to direct airflow) or configuration (ie. nose or belly camera removal exposing an opening in the fuselage). Therefore, this configuration is usually not recommended, especially if airspeed sensors are being used during an autolanding."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-pitot-considerations.rst:49
#: 016cf8bc7d4145d9ba2bb0acba8b55b4
msgid "The impact of angle of attack on the cabin pressure (and by inference airspeed reading accuracy)can be checked by examining the BARO.Alt vs GPS.Alt log readings at various speeds, since the BARO is measuring altitude based on cabin pressure."
msgstr ""
